            'By moving their head backward as they are struck, a boxer increases the time over which the punch brings their head to a stop (or slows its forward motion). Since the impulse (change in momentum) delivered by the punch is largely fixed by the punch itself, increasing the time over which that impulse acts reduces the average force experienced by the boxer’s head (F = Δp ÷ Δt), reducing the risk of injury compared to keeping the head rigid and still.',
