          prompt: 'Explain the difference between structural isomers and the same compound drawn in two different orientations, using an example.',
          answer:
            'Structural isomers are genuinely different compounds that happen to share the same molecular formula, but differ in how their atoms are actually connected (bonded) to each other — for example, butane and 2-methylpropane, which have a different arrangement of carbon-carbon bonds. Drawing the same compound in a different orientation (for example, writing CH₃−CH₂−OH the same way but flipped left-to-right, or drawn as a zig-zag versus a straight line) does not create a new isomer, since the actual pattern of atomic connections (which atom is bonded to which) remains completely unchanged — it is simply a different visual representation of the identical molecule.',
