          prompt: 'Distinguish between an addition reaction and a substitution reaction, using the reaction of ethene with bromine (addition) and the reaction of methane with bromine under UV light (substitution) as examples.',
          answer:
            'In an addition reaction, two reactant molecules combine to form a single product molecule, with no atoms lost — for example, ethene (CH₂=CH₂) reacts with bromine (Br₂) by the double bond "opening up" and a bromine atom adding to each carbon, forming 1,2-dibromoethane, with no other product formed. In a substitution reaction, one atom (or group of atoms) in a molecule is replaced by another, with a second small molecule released as a by-product — for example, methane (CH₄) reacts with bromine under UV light by one hydrogen atom being replaced by a bromine atom, forming bromomethane (CH₃Br) and releasing hydrogen bromide (HBr) as a by-product.',
