          prompt: 'Explain how a graph of gas volume produced against time could be used to compare the average rate of two experiments (X and Y) that reach the same final volume of gas, if experiment X reaches its final volume in less time than experiment Y.',
          answer:
            'Since both experiments produce the same total (final) volume of gas, but experiment X reaches this volume in a shorter time than experiment Y, experiment X’s graph will be steeper (rise more quickly) than experiment Y’s graph over the period before completion. Because average rate = Δvolume ÷ Δt, and both experiments have the same Δvolume (final volume) but experiment X has a smaller Δt to reach it, experiment X has a higher average rate than experiment Y.',
          explanation: 'This illustrates a general way to compare reaction rates directly from a graph: for the same total amount of product, whichever curve reaches its final (plateau) value in less time represents the faster average reaction rate.',
