          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain why increasing temperature increases reaction rate by a larger factor than a similarly proportioned increase in concentration typically does, referring to the distribution of particle energies.',
          answer:
            'Increasing concentration increases the frequency of collisions in a roughly proportional way, without changing the proportion of particles that have sufficient energy to react. Increasing temperature, by contrast, shifts the entire distribution of particle kinetic energies (the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution) toward higher energies, which disproportionately increases the number of particles with energy above the activation energy threshold (since this threshold sits toward the higher-energy tail of the distribution, where even a small shift in the distribution can produce a comparatively large increase in the number of particles exceeding it). Because a modest temperature increase can substantially increase both the collision frequency and, more significantly, the proportion of energetically successful collisions, its overall effect on rate tends to be much larger than an equivalent proportional increase in concentration.',
          explanation: 'This is why, in practice, even a relatively small increase in temperature (such as 10 °C) can roughly double a typical reaction rate, an effect far larger than what a similar percentage increase in reactant concentration alone would produce.',
