          prompt: 'Explain why breaking a chemical bond always requires an input of energy.',
          answer: 'A chemical bond forms because it lowers the overall energy of the bonded atoms compared to being separate (bonding is energetically favourable); breaking that same bond therefore requires an input of energy exactly equal to the bond energy, to overcome this favourable, lower-energy bonded state and separate the atoms once more.',
          explanation: 'This is the underlying reason bond breaking is always an energy-absorbing (endothermic) step, while bond formation is always an energy-releasing (exothermic) step — this remains true regardless of whether the overall reaction (combining several bond-breaking and bond-forming steps) is exothermic or endothermic.',
