          answer: 'Activation energy represents the energy needed to reach the highest-energy point (the transition state) of the reaction pathway, starting from the reactants. Since the transition state is, by definition, always at a higher energy than the reactants (an energy "hump" must always be climbed before any reaction can occur), the energy required to reach it from the reactants is always a positive value, regardless of whether the reactants ultimately end up at a lower energy (exothermic) or higher energy (endothermic) than they started.',
          explanation: 'This is why every reaction, even a strongly exothermic one that releases a large amount of net energy overall, still requires some initial energy input (the activation energy) to actually get started — a reaction with a very high activation energy can be extremely slow at room temperature, even if it is strongly exothermic overall.',
