          topicId: 'phys-types-of-reactions',
          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe the observations you would expect to make when a strip of zinc metal is added to dilute sulfuric acid, and explain these observations in terms of the redox reaction taking place.',
          answer:
            'The zinc strip would be seen to fizz, gradually dissolving/shrinking in size, with bubbles of a colourless gas being produced, and the reaction mixture may feel warm to the touch. These observations occur because zinc reacts with the acid in a redox reaction (Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂), in which zinc is oxidised (losing electrons to become Zn²⁺, which dissolves into solution, causing the strip to shrink) while hydrogen ions are reduced to hydrogen gas (H₂), which is released as the observed bubbles; the reaction is also exothermic, explaining the release of heat.',
          explanation: 'This links the directly observable, macroscopic changes (fizzing, dissolving metal, gas bubbles, warmth) to the underlying particle-level redox process (electron transfer between zinc and hydrogen ions) and the reaction’s exothermic nature.',
