          label: '6.6',
          topicId: 'phys-types-of-reactions',
          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain the difference between a "strong" base and a "concentrated" base, using ammonia (a weak base) as an example to illustrate that these two terms describe different properties.',
          answer:
            'Strength refers to the extent to which a base ionises (or reacts with water to produce OH⁻ ions): a strong base ionises almost completely, while a weak base (such as ammonia) only reacts partially with water, regardless of how much of it is dissolved. Concentration refers to the amount (moles) of the base dissolved per unit volume of solution, independent of how much of that dissolved base actually ionises or reacts. This means it is entirely possible to have a concentrated solution of a weak base (a large amount of ammonia dissolved in a small volume of water, even though only a small fraction of it reacts with water to form OH⁻ ions at any time) or a dilute solution of a strong base (a small amount of a strong base, such as NaOH, dissolved in a large volume of water, with that small amount still ionising almost completely).',
          explanation: 'This distinction is a common area of confusion: strength (a fixed chemical property of a particular base, related to how completely it ionises or reacts with water) and concentration (a variable property of a particular solution, related to how much base was dissolved) are independent of one another, and a substance can be strong-and-dilute, strong-and-concentrated, weak-and-dilute, or weak-and-concentrated.',
