          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain how total internal reflection enables a medical endoscope to transmit a clear image of the inside of the body along a bundle of thin optical fibres.',
          answer:
            'An optical fibre consists of a core with a high refractive index, surrounded by a cladding layer with a lower refractive index. Light carrying the image enters the fibre and strikes the boundary between the core and cladding at an angle greater than the critical angle for that pair of materials, causing it to undergo total internal reflection and reflect back into the core rather than escaping into the cladding. This repeats continuously along the length of each thin, flexible fibre in the bundle, so light (and the image information it carries) can travel around bends inside the body and emerge intact at the far end, where it is reconstructed into a viewable image.',
          explanation: 'Because total internal reflection reflects essentially all of the light back into the core at each bounce, very little light (and therefore very little image detail) is lost even along a long, flexible, repeatedly bent bundle of fibres, which is what makes endoscopy possible.',
