          prompt: 'Describe what happens to the diffraction pattern produced when a wave passes through a slit that is much WIDER than its wavelength, compared to a slit whose width is close to the wavelength.',
          answer: 'When the slit is much wider than the wavelength, the wave shows very little diffraction — it continues travelling mostly in a straight line, with only a slight, barely noticeable spreading near the edges of the slit. When the slit width is close to the wavelength, the wave diffracts strongly, spreading out noticeably into the region behind the slit.',
          explanation: 'This confirms that the amount of diffraction depends on the size of the gap relative to the wavelength: a slit that is large compared to the wavelength behaves almost like an open boundary with negligible spreading, while a slit comparable in size to the wavelength produces pronounced spreading.',
