          answer:
            'Ammonia molecules can form hydrogen bonds with each other, since hydrogen is bonded directly to nitrogen (one of the three elements, along with O and F, capable of hydrogen bonding). The other Group 15 hydrides (PH₃, AsH₃, SbH₃) cannot form hydrogen bonds, since phosphorus, arsenic and antimony are not electronegative or small enough to support this type of bonding, and instead rely only on the weaker dipole-dipole and van der Waals forces. Because hydrogen bonding is considerably stronger than these other intermolecular forces, ammonia requires more energy to boil than its low molar mass alone would predict, breaking the otherwise expected trend.',
          explanation: 'This is a classic example used to demonstrate the exceptional strength of hydrogen bonding: ammonia has a much lower molar mass than PH₃, AsH₃ or SbH₃, yet has a higher boiling point than PH₃, purely because of this additional hydrogen-bonding contribution absent in the other Group 15 hydrides.',
