          answer: 'When the gap is much smaller than the wavelength, the wavefronts emerging on the far side spread out strongly in a nearly circular pattern, resembling waves from a single point source. As the gap width is increased toward and beyond the size of the wavelength, this spreading becomes progressively less pronounced, until, once the gap is much wider than the wavelength, the emerging wavefronts remain almost entirely flat and straight, with only a slight spreading visible near the edges of the gap.',
          explanation: 'This confirms the general diffraction principle: the amount by which a wave spreads on passing through a gap depends on the size of the gap relative to the wavelength, with the most pronounced diffraction occurring when the two are comparable in size.',
