          prompt: 'A loudspeaker around the corner of a building produces both low-pitched (bass) and high-pitched (treble) sound at the same time. Explain, in terms of diffraction, why a listener standing around the corner hears the bass notes more clearly than the treble notes.',
          answer:
            'Bass (low-frequency) sound has a long wavelength, which is comparable in size to the corner of the building, so it diffracts strongly around the corner, allowing much of it to reach the listener even without a direct line of sight to the speaker. Treble (high-frequency) sound has a much shorter wavelength, considerably smaller relative to the size of the corner, so it diffracts far less and tends to continue travelling in a straighter line past the corner, meaning much less of it reaches the listener standing in the "shadow" region around the corner.',
          explanation: 'This is a direct consequence of the general diffraction principle: the amount of diffraction depends on the wavelength relative to the size of the obstacle or gap, which is why bass-heavy sound is often described as travelling "through walls" more easily than higher-pitched sound.',
