          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Using Huygens’ Principle, explain why decreasing the width of a single narrow slit (while keeping the wavelength of the wave constant) causes the diffraction pattern behind the slit to spread out over a wider angle.',
          answer:
            'According to Huygens’ Principle, every point along the section of the original wavefront that passes through the slit acts as a source of new secondary wavelets, spreading out in all directions. As the slit is made narrower, a progressively smaller section of the original wavefront — and therefore fewer adjacent secondary sources — passes through it, so these secondary wavelets have fewer neighbouring wavelets nearby to interfere with and partially cancel their sideways-spreading components. With very few secondary sources active (in the limit, almost a single point), the wavelets spread out strongly in nearly all directions on the far side, producing a diffraction pattern that spreads over a much wider angle than would be produced by a wider slit, where many adjacent secondary sources combine to largely preserve the original, narrower forward-travelling wavefront.',
          explanation: 'This explains why diffraction becomes more noticeable as the ratio of wavelength to slit width increases: a very narrow slit relative to the wavelength isolates so few secondary sources that the wave essentially radiates outward like a single point source.',
