          answer:
            'A compass needle naturally aligns itself with the local magnetic field. A current-carrying wire produces its own circular magnetic field around it (as given by the right-hand rule), and close to the wire this field can be much stronger than the Earth’s magnetic field, so the needle instead aligns with the direction of the wire’s field at that point, deflecting it away from north. If the direction of the current is reversed, the direction of the circular magnetic field around the wire reverses (by the right-hand rule), so the needle deflects toward the opposite side instead.',
