          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'A stiff, straight, current-carrying conducting rod rests on two horizontal conducting rails within a uniform external magnetic field directed vertically upward (a simple "current balance" demonstration). Explain why the rod experiences a horizontal force when current flows through it, and describe TWO ways the magnitude of this force could be increased.',
          answer:
            'The rod carries a current, which is a flow of moving charge; since these moving charges lie within an external magnetic field, they experience a magnetic force (the motor effect), and because this applies to the charges all along the rod, the combined effect is a net force on the rod itself, directed horizontally along the rails (its direction found using the right-hand rule, relating the current direction and the field direction to the resulting force direction). The magnitude of this force can be increased by (1) increasing the current flowing through the rod, or (2) using a stronger external magnetic field (for example, by moving the magnets producing the field closer together).',
          explanation: 'This "current balance" arrangement is a direct, simplified demonstration of the same motor effect that drives an electric motor — a current-carrying conductor experiencing a force while situated in an external magnetic field — and is sometimes used experimentally to determine the strength of a magnetic field, since the size of the resulting force depends on it.',
