          prompt: 'Describe what is represented by the peak of the curve on a reaction’s energy (activation-energy) diagram, and name this point or species.',
          answer:
            'The peak represents the point of highest energy reached during the reaction, corresponding to the activated complex (also called the transition state) — a temporary, unstable, high-energy arrangement of atoms partway between the reactants and the products, in which the original bonds are partially broken and the new bonds are partially formed. The height of this peak above the level of the reactants represents the activation energy of the reaction.',
          explanation: 'The activated complex is never actually isolated or observed directly, since it exists only for an extremely brief instant during a successful collision between reactant particles, before rapidly proceeding on to form the products.',
