          prompt: 'State ONE difference between a plane (straight) wavefront and a circular wavefront, with reference to the source producing each.',
          answer: 'A plane wavefront is straight/flat, with parallel rays, and is produced by a source that is very far away (or effectively an infinitely long, straight source). A circular wavefront (a spherical wavefront in three dimensions) is produced by a single point source, with rays radiating outward in all directions from that point.',
          explanation: 'A plane wavefront can, in fact, be thought of as a small section of a circular wavefront produced by a source so far away that its curvature is no longer noticeable over that small section, which is why distant sources (such as the Sun) produce light that reaches Earth as essentially plane wavefronts.',
