          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'A wave of constant speed passes through a gap of fixed width. Describe how the diffraction pattern produced changes as the FREQUENCY of the wave is increased.',
          answer: 'Increasing the frequency of the wave, while its speed stays constant, causes its wavelength to decrease (since v = fλ, and v is fixed). As the wavelength becomes smaller relative to the fixed gap width, the amount of diffraction (spreading) of the wave on passing through the gap decreases.',
          explanation: 'This shows that diffraction depends on wavelength, not frequency, directly — frequency only matters here because of its inverse relationship with wavelength at constant wave speed.',
