          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain why an old analogue television antenna needed a precise, direct line of sight to the transmitter to receive a clear picture, whereas an AM radio — using a much lower frequency (longer wavelength) signal — could often still receive a signal without a direct line of sight, such as behind a hill or inside a building.',
          answer:
            'Analogue television signals use a relatively high frequency (short wavelength), which is small compared to the size of typical obstacles such as hills and buildings, so the signal diffracts very little around such obstacles, requiring a clear, direct line of sight to the transmitter for reliable reception. AM radio signals use a much lower frequency (considerably longer wavelength), which is comparable in size to (or even larger than) many everyday obstacles, so the signal diffracts much more readily around hills and into buildings, allowing reception even without a direct line of sight to the transmitter.',
          explanation: 'This is a direct consequence of the general diffraction principle: the amount of diffraction depends on the size of the wavelength relative to the size of the obstacle, which is why lower-frequency (longer-wavelength) radio signals are generally better suited to covering an area with many obstacles than higher-frequency signals.',
