          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Using Huygens’ Principle, explain why the wavefronts of a water wave spread out (curve) around the edge of a breakwater wall in a harbour, so that some wave energy is detected in the "shadow" region directly behind the wall, rather than that region remaining completely calm.',
          answer:
            'According to Huygens’ Principle, every point along a wavefront acts as a source of new secondary wavelets, spreading outward in all directions. As the wavefronts travel past the end of the breakwater wall, the points on the wavefront immediately beside its edge continue to act as secondary sources, and the wavelets they produce spread sideways and inward into the region directly behind the wall, rather than simply stopping at the edge of the geometric shadow the wall would cast. This causes water waves — and the wave energy they carry — to be detected at some distance into the region behind the wall, with the extent of this spreading being greater when the wavelength of the waves is larger relative to the size (or the gap at the end) of the wall.',
          explanation: 'This is the same underlying mechanism responsible for diffraction through a narrow gap: an edge (or a gap) provides secondary sources of wavelets adjacent to it, which spread into a region a purely straight-line (ray) picture would incorrectly predict to be completely undisturbed.',
