          answer:
            'When the plane of the coil is parallel to the external magnetic field, the motor-effect forces on the two current-carrying sides of the coil (which carry current in opposite directions to each other) act perpendicular to the plane of the coil, at the greatest possible perpendicular distance (moment arm) from the coil’s axis of rotation — producing the maximum possible turning moment (torque) about that axis. When the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field, the forces on the two sides instead act directly along the plane of the coil, effectively along the same line as the radius arm connecting each side to the axis (pulling directly outward from, or pushing directly toward, the axis) rather than perpendicular to it, so they produce no turning moment about the axis at that instant, and the torque is momentarily zero.',
