          answer:
            'This statement is incorrect because strength and concentration are two entirely independent properties of an acid solution. Strength refers to the extent to which an acid ionises (dissociates) in water — a fixed chemical property of a particular acid: a strong acid ionises almost completely, while a weak acid only ionises partially, regardless of how much of it is dissolved. Concentration refers to the amount (moles) of acid dissolved per unit volume of solution — a variable property of a particular solution, independent of how much of that dissolved acid actually ionises. For example, a very dilute solution of a strong acid (such as 0.001 mol·dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid) can easily be LESS concentrated than a concentrated solution of a weak acid (such as 5 mol·dm⁻³ ethanoic acid), showing that a strong acid is not necessarily more concentrated than a weak acid at all — a solution can correctly be described as strong-and-dilute, strong-and-concentrated, weak-and-dilute, or weak-and-concentrated.',
