          prompt: 'Explain why sound waves diffract noticeably around a corner, allowing a person to hear a conversation happening around the corner, while light passing the same corner shows no noticeable diffraction and instead casts a sharp shadow.',
          answer:
            'Sound waves have a relatively long wavelength (typically centimetres to metres), often comparable in size to everyday obstacles such as a wall or corner, so they diffract noticeably around such obstacles, allowing sound to reach a listener who is not in a direct line of sight of the source. Visible light has a very short wavelength (a tiny fraction of a millimetre), far smaller than the size of a typical wall or corner, so it shows no observable diffraction around such large obstacles, and instead simply travels in straight lines, casting a sharp shadow.',
          explanation: 'This is a direct consequence of the general diffraction principle: the amount of diffraction observed depends on the size of the obstacle or gap relative to the wavelength, not on the type of wave itself — light of a much shorter wavelength (such as in a laser) would diffract noticeably around a suitably small enough obstacle or through a suitably narrow enough gap.',
