          marks: 3,
          prompt: 'Explain why household electrical appliances are typically connected in parallel with one another, rather than in series.',
          answer: 'Connecting appliances in parallel means each appliance is connected directly across the same full mains voltage, so each operates normally at its rated voltage regardless of how many other appliances are also switched on or off, and any single appliance can be switched off (or fail) without affecting the operation of the others. If appliances were connected in series instead, they would share the same single current, the total voltage would be divided unevenly between them depending on each appliance’s resistance, and switching off (or the failure of) any one appliance would break the circuit and switch off every other appliance in that series chain as well.',
